n 2 | ==============================\n 3 | Lasso on dense and sparse data\n 4 | ==============================\n 5 | \n 6 | We show that linear_model.Lasso provides the same results for dense and sparse\n 7 | data and that in the case of sparse data the speed is improved.\n 8 | \n 9 | \"\"\"\n10 | \n11 | from time import time\n12 | from scipy import sparse\n13 | from scipy import linalg\n14 | \n15 | from sklearn.datasets import make_regression\n16 | from sklearn.linear_model import Lasso\n17 | \n18 | \n19 | # %%\n20 | # Comparing the two Lasso implementations on Dense data\n21 | # -----------------------------------------------------\n22 | #\n23 | # We create a linear regression problem that is suitable for the Lasso,\n24 | # that is to say, with more features than samples. We then store the data\n25 | # matrix in both dense (the usual) and sparse format, and train a Lasso on\n26 | # each. We compute the runtime of both and check that they learned the\n27 | # same model by computing the Euclidean norm of the difference between the\n28 | # coefficients they learned. Because the data is dense, we expect better\n29 | # runtime with a dense data format.\n30 | \n31 | X, y = make_regression(n_samples=200, n_features=5000, random_state=0)\n32 | # create a copy of X in sparse format\n33 | X_sp = sparse.coo_matrix(X)\n34 | \n35 | alpha = 1\n36 | sparse_lasso = Lasso(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, max_iter=1000)\n37 | dense_lasso = Lasso(alpha=alpha, fit_intercept=False, max_iter=1000)\n38 | \n39 | t0 = time()\n40 | sparse_lasso.fit(X_sp, y)\n41 | print(f\"Sparse Lasso done in {(time() - t0):.3f}s\")\n42 | \n43 | t0 = time()\n44 | dense_lasso.fit(X, y)\n45 | print(f\"Dense Lasso done in {(time() - t0):.3f}s\")\n46 | \n47 | # compare the regression coefficients\n48 | coeff_diff = linalg.norm(sparse_lasso.coef_ - dense_lasso.coef_)\n49 | print(f\"Distance between coefficients : {coeff_diff:.2e}\")\n50 | \n51 | #\n52 | # %%\n53 | # Comparing the two Lasso implementations on Sparse data\n54 | # ------------------------------------------------------\n55 | #\n56 | # We make the previous problem sparse by replacing all small values with 0\n57 | # and run the same comparisons as above. Because the data is now sparse, we\n58 | # expect the implementation that uses the sparse data format to be faster.\n59 | \n60 | # make a copy of the previous data\n61 | Xs = X.copy()\n62 | # make Xs sparse by replacing the values lower than 2.5 with 0s\n63 | Xs[Xs < 2.5] = 0.0\n64 | # create a copy of Xs in sparse format\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:paired_distances`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1165 | def paired_distances(X, Y, *, metric=\"euclidean\", **kwds):\n1166 |     \"\"\"\n1167 |     Compute the paired distances between X and Y.\n1168 | \n1169 |     Compute the distances between (X[0], Y[0]), (X[1], Y[1]), etc...\n1170 | \n1171 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <metrics>`.\n1172 | \n1173 |     Parameters\n1174 |     ----------\n1175 |     X : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1176 |         Array 1 for distance computation.\n1177 | \n1178 |     Y : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1179 |         Array 2 for distance computation.\n1180 | \n1181 |     metric : str or callable, default=\"euclidean\"\n1182 |         The metric to use when calculating distance between instances in a\n1183 |         feature array. If metric is a string, it must be one of the options\n1184 |         specified in PAIRED_DISTANCES, including \"euclidean\",\n1185 |         \"manhattan\", or \"cosine\".\n1186 |         Alternatively, if metric is a callable function, it is called on each\n1187 |         pair of instances (rows) and the resulting value recorded. The callable\n1188 |         should take two arrays from `X` as input and return a value indicating\n1189 |         the distance between them.\n1190 | \n1191 |     **kwds : dict\n1192 |         Unused parameters.\n1193 | \n1194 |     Returns\n1195 |     -------\n1196 |     distances : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n1197 |         Returns the distances between the row vectors of `X`\n1198 |         and the row vectors of `Y`.\n1199 | \n1200 |     See Also\n1201 |     --------\n1202 |     pairwise_distances : Computes the distance between every pair of samples.\n1203 | \n1204 |     Examples\n1205 |     --------\n1206 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import paired_distances\n1207 |     >>> X = [[0, 1], [1, 1]]\n1208 |     >>> Y = [[0, 1], [2, 1]]\n1209 |     >>> paired_distances(X, Y)\n1210 |     array([0., 1.])\n1211 |     \"\"\"\n1212 | \n1213 |     if metric in PAIRED_DISTANCES:\n1214 |         func = PAIRED_DISTANCES[metric]\n1215 |         return func(X, Y)\n1216 |     elif callable(metric):\n1217 |         # Check the matrix first (it is usually done by the metric)\n1218 |         X, Y = check_paired_arrays(X, Y)\n1219 |         distances = np.zeros(len(X))\n1220 |         for i in range(len(X)):\n1221 |             distances[i] = metric(X[i], Y[i])\n1222 |         return distances\n1223 |     else:\n1224 |         raise ValueError(\"Unknown distance %s\" % metric)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:linear_kernel`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1235 | def linear_kernel(X, Y=None, dense_output=True):\n1236 |     \"\"\"\n1237 |     Compute the linear kernel between X and Y.\n1238 | \n1239 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <linear_kernel>`.\n1240 | \n1241 |     Parameters\n1242 |     ----------\n1243 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples_X, n_features)\n1244 |         A feature array.\n1245 | \n1246 |     Y : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples_Y, n_features), default=None\n1247 |         An optional second feature array. If `None`, uses `Y=X`.\n1248 | \n1249 |     dense_output : bool, default=True\n1250 |         Whether to return dense output even when the input is sparse. If\n1251 |         ``False